<section>
  <h2>Worked example</h2>
  <p>
    Using <i>u</i> = 20 m/s, θ = 45°, <i>h</i> = 0, and <i>g</i> = 9.81 m/s²:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><i>u</i> cos 45° = <i>u</i> sin 45° = 14.14 m/s</li>
    <li>Time of flight <i>T</i> = 2 × 14.14 / 9.81 = 2.88 s</li>
    <li>Range <i>R</i> = 14.14 × 2.88 = 40.77 m</li>
    <li>Greatest height <i>H</i> = 14.14² / (2 × 9.81) = 10.19 m</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    These match the live values at the default settings. Now take the same
    speed on level ground at θ = 30°.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><i>u</i> cos 30° = 17.32 m/s, <i>u</i> sin 30° = 10 m/s</li>
    <li><i>T</i> = 2 × 10 / 9.81 = 2.04 s</li>
    <li><i>R</i> = 17.32 × 2.04 = 35.31 m, and <i>H</i> = 100 / 19.62 = 5.10 m</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Lower the angle to see the range fall from the 45° maximum. Raise
    <i>h</i> to project from a cliff — <i>T</i> and <i>R</i> both grow.
  </p>
</section>
